06 — Splunk SIEM Setup

What is this for?
Splunk is a SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) platform. It collects, indexes, and searches log data from across the homelab. This allows you to monitor security events, investigate incidents, and practice real SOC (Security Operations Center) skills. Splunk is installed directly on its own dedicated Ubuntu Server VM.

Prerequisites
A second Ubuntu Server VM is set up (see 02 Ubuntu Server Setup)
The VM has at least 4GB RAM and 30GB disk — Splunk is resource heavy
You are SSH'd into the second Ubuntu Server VM

Downloading Splunk Enterprise
Go to https://www.splunk.com and create a free account if you do not have one
Navigate to Products → Free Trials & Downloads → Splunk Enterprise
Select the Linux .deb package and copy the wget download link

On your VM, download Splunk:

   wget -O splunk.deb 'paste-your-download-link-here'

Installing Splunk
Install the package:

   sudo dpkg -i splunk.deb

Start Splunk and accept the license:

   sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk start --accept-license

On first start it will ask you to create an admin username and password — remember these.

Enable Splunk to Start on Boot

   sudo /opt/splunk/bin/splunk enable boot-start

Accessing Splunk
Open a browser and navigate to:

   http://10.0.0.x:8000

Replace 10.0.0.x with the IP of your Splunk VM.
Log in with the admin credentials you created during install.

Setting Up Log Ingestion
Splunk collects logs via Universal Forwarders installed on other machines, or by monitoring local log files.

Adding a Receiving Port (on Splunk server)
In the Splunk web UI go to Settings → Forwarding and Receiving
Under Receive Data click Configure Receiving → New Receiving Port
Set the port to 9997 and save

Installing the Universal Forwarder on DarwinPC (Windows)
Download the Universal Forwarder from https://www.splunk.com/en_us/download/universal-forwarder.html
Run the installer and select:
   Deployment Server    leave blank
   Receiving Indexer    10.0.0.x port 9997 (your Splunk VM IP)
During install select which Windows logs to forward (Security, System, Application)

Installing the Universal Forwarder on Ubuntu Server VM
On your first Ubuntu Server VM, run:

   wget -O splunkforwarder.deb 'paste-your-forwarder-download-link-here'
   sudo dpkg -i splunkforwarder.deb
   sudo /opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk start --accept-license
   sudo /opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk add forward-server 10.0.0.x:9997 -auth admin:yourpassword
   sudo /opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk add monitor /var/log/auth.log -index main -sourcetype auth
   sudo /opt/splunkforwarder/bin/splunk enable boot-start

Replace 10.0.0.x with your Splunk VM IP and yourpassword with your Splunk admin password.

Verifying Data is Coming In
In the Splunk web UI go to Search & Reporting
Run the following search:

   index=main | stats count by host

You should see your hosts (DarwinPC, ubuntuserver) listed with event counts.

Searching Logs
Basic search for failed Windows logins:

   index=main EventCode=4625

Search for SSH activity on the Ubuntu server:

   index=main source="/var/log/auth.log"

Search for all critical events in the last 24 hours:

   index=main earliest=-24h | stats count by EventCode, host

